.TH EUCLID-CLI 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli \- command-line client for a Euclid installation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fIglobal-options\fR]
.I module action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.PP
.B euclid-cli
.IR module " help"
.PP
.B euclid-cli
.I module action
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B euclid-cli
is the command-line client for Euclid. Every command names a
.I module
and an
.I action
on it \- \fBeuclid-cli esm list-buckets\fR, \fBeuclid-cli eqs
send-message\fR \- which mirrors how the installation itself is built:
each module is a separate service behind the gateway, and an action is
one request to it.
.PP
A command sends a single JSON request to the gateway
(\fB\-\-endpoint\fR, default \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR) and prints
the JSON response, so its output can be piped into
.BR jq (1)
or any other JSON reader. The exceptions are the ESM transfer actions,
which stream raw bytes rather than JSON in one direction \- see
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-esm-download-file (1).
.PP
There is no command that changes anything locally: the CLI holds a
session and nothing else, so anything it can do can equally be done by
an SDK or by an HTTP client that signs its requests the same way.
.SH MODULES
.TP
.B eam
Access management: users, groups, accounts, namespaces, and the login
that every other module's session comes from. See
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.TP
.B esm
Storage: buckets, objects, uploads and downloads, object attributes, and
subscriptions that notify a queue or topic. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1).
.TP
.B eqs
Queueing: queues and messages. See
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1).
.TP
.B ens
Notifications: publish/subscribe topics and their messages. See
.BR euclid-cli-ens (1).
.TP
.B ees
Events: subscribe to what modules publish, and receive it durably
without a queue of your own. See
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1).
.TP
.B ekm
Key management: cryptographic keys, encryption and decryption. See
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1).
.TP
.B emm
Module management: what the manager is running, and export/import of a
module's collections. See
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1).
.TP
.B ets
Transfer servers: FTP and SFTP endpoints onto an ESM bucket. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1).
.TP
.B eap
Applications: Java, Python, Node.js, Rust or C++ processes that Euclid
runs and scales. See
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options are recognized only
.I before
the module name. The first token that is not one of them (or a value for
one) starts \fImodule action \fR[\fIaction-options\fR...], which is
passed on verbatim \- so an action option never has to be escaped to
keep the CLI from claiming it.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to. Defaults to
.IR https://localhost:5566 .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, for the self-signed certificate a development installation
generates. Defaults to
.IR /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid_cert.crt .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
JSON configuration file providing defaults for action options \- see
.B CONFIGURATION
below. Silently ignored if it does not exist, since the CLI is often run
from a machine that has no installation of its own. Defaults to
.IR /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid.json .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature " " \fISCHEME\fR
Signature scheme for signed service calls:
.I sigv4
(the default) or
.I rfc9421
for RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures. Both prove the request with the
same access key; RFC 9421 puts the signature in
.IR Signature / Signature\-Input
headers and binds the body through
.I Content\-Digest
(RFC 9530) instead of using AWS's Authorization header. Overrides
.I euclid.cli.signature
from the configuration file. Ignored for
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1)
commands, which authenticate with the session's bearer token.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty " " \fIBOOL\fR
Pretty-print the JSON response. Defaults to \fItrue\fR; pass
\fB\-\-pretty 0\fR for single-line output.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the module list, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH HELP
Every module and every action documents itself, which is the fastest way
to find an option's exact name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm help              list the module's actions
euclid-cli esm put-object help   one action's synopsis and options
euclid-cli esm put-object --help the same thing
.fi
.RE
.PP
Help never requires a session, so it works before logging in.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
authenticates against the access module and writes the session to
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials ,
which every later command reads. One login per machine is enough until
the session expires or the namespace is changed.
.PP
The session holds two things: a bearer token, used for
.B eam
commands, and a SigV4 access key provisioned on first login, used to
sign every other module's requests. Which of the two is used is decided
per module, not per command, so nothing has to be passed on the command
line.
.PP
Some modules are administrator-only \-
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
among them, since they decide what runs and which network listeners
exist. The CLI checks this before sending, for a clearer message than a
bare 401; the server checks independently on every request and is the
actual boundary.
.PP
If a command fails with
.IR "HTTP 401" ,
the session has expired: run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The file named by
.B \-\-config
supplies defaults for options that would otherwise be typed on every
invocation:
.TP
.I euclid.cli.signature
Signature scheme, as for
.BR \-\-signature .
.TP
.IR euclid.modules.storage.part-size ", " euclid.modules.storage.concurrency
Part size and parallelism used by
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-esm-download-file (1),
so a deployment can tune transfers once rather than per command.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the module or action is unknown, there is no
session or it lacks the required privileges, the request could not be
sent, or the server rejected it. Details are printed to standard error,
so standard output stays parseable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
The current session: bearer token, SigV4 access key, user, account,
region and active namespace. Written by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every other command.
.TP
.I /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid.json
Default configuration file; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.TP
.I /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid_cert.crt
Default additional CA certificate; see
.BR \-\-ca\-cert .
.SH EXAMPLES
Log in, then store and read back a file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eam login --user admin --password admin --namespace development
euclid-cli esm create-bucket --name inbox
euclid-cli esm upload-file \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:inbox \e
    --key reports/q3.csv --file ./q3.csv
euclid-cli esm list-objects \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:inbox
.fi
.RE
.PP
Talk to a remote installation with a self-signed certificate, printing
compact JSON for a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://euclid.example.com:5566 \e
    --ca-cert ./euclid_cert.crt --pretty 0 \e
    eqs list-queues | jq -r '.queues[].name'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-ftp (8),
.BR euclid-sftp (8)
